      "text": "To find the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we start by using the formula for the projection matrix onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T}{\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). First, we compute \\(\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute \\(\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a} = (-3)^2 + (-2)^2 = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(\\mathbf{P}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is a projection matrix, it is idempotent, meaning \\(\\mathbf{P}^2 = \\mathbf{P}\\). Therefore, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) does not exist, and the operation \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) is not defined.\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The inverse does not exist}}\n\\]",
