      "text": "Since the projection onto a non-zero vector must be onto that vector, then the matrix\n$\\mathbf{P} = \\text{proj}_{\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}} = \\frac{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}}{\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}} \\cdot \\mathbf{1} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2\\end{pmatrix}$\nand the inverse does not exist!\n\nYour solution for projecting onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is correct. To find \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), we need to recognize that since the projection matrix onto a single vector vector has a rank of 1 (i.e., it is not invertible), its inverse does not exist.\n\nTo verify:\n1. Any binary result \\(b \\cdot a\\) in a scalar operation, here the inner product calculation, will never lead to a division by zero scenario in the vector projection formula because it involves non-zero vectors.\n2. Rank of the projection matrix (\\(1\\)): The projection matrix is rank deficient because it produces only one unique vector. In matrix form, if the rank of the projection is less than the rank of the column space vector, the inverse of this projection matrix does not exist due to lacking a defined non-zero determinant matrix.\n\nTherefore, labeling \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) as the projection matrix onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is essentially ill-defined due to the rank issue. Therefore, its inverse does not exist.\n\nThus, the final response is the zero matrix \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
