      "text": "To solve for \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), we need to determine the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto the vector \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector onto \\(\\mathbf{a}\\). The formula for the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T}{\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}}\n\\]\nWe first compute \\(\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nNext, we compute \\(\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a} = (-3)^2 + (-2)^2 = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\nThus, the projection matrix is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nTo find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\), we need to check if \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is invertible. A matrix is invertible if and only if its determinant is non-zero. The determinant of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{P}) = \\left( \\frac{9}{13} \\right) \\left( \\frac{4}{13} \\right) - \\left( \\frac{6}{13} \\right) \\left( \\frac{6}{13} \\right) = \\frac{36}{169} - \\frac{36}{169} = 0\n\\]\nSince the determinant is zero, \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is not invertible. Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) does not exist, and we write the zero matrix:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
